1.  Introduction

   Standard English first-person pronouns distinguish one speaker ("I")
   from a group that includes the speaker ("we").  They do not mark
   whether the work being described was produced through meaningful
   interaction with an artificial intelligence (AI) system.

   Increasingly, human work is produced in exactly that way.  In such
   cases, neither "I" nor "we" necessarily communicates the structure
   of the authorship or agency accurately.  This document introduces
   two pronouns, "wai" and "wei", that make this form of collaboration
   linguistically visible.  The goal is not to redefine humanity,
   personhood, or responsibility.

   This document is intended for AI practitioners, developers,
   researchers, writers, designers, standards communities, and users of
   human-AI collaborative systems.

1.1.  Motivation

   "I wrote the program" means that one speaker claims authorship.
   "We wrote the program" means that the speaker belongs to a group
   responsible for the work.

   AI-assisted production creates additional cases.  A developer may
   define a system architecture, discuss implementation approaches with
   an AI system, generate code through the AI, reject portions of that
   code, debug the result, request alternative solutions, test them,
   and ultimately determine the final implementation.

   Saying "I coded this program" may conceal a meaningful aspect of the
   production process.  Saying "We coded this program" may incorrectly
   suggest that the speaker is referring to multiple humans or to
   conventional collaborators.  A similar ambiguity exists when an
   entire human team collaborates extensively with AI.

1.2.  Transparency

   One objective of this proposal is to make meaningful AI
   participation more visible.  Compare:

      I produced this analysis.
      Wai produced this analysis.

   The second form communicates additional information about the
   production process in a single word.  Similarly, "We designed the
   system" and "Wei designed the system" distinguish a conventional
   human team from an AI-integrated team.

1.3.  Requirements Language

   The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT",
   "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and
   "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in
   BCP 14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and only when, they appear in all
   capitals, as shown here.

   Because this document specifies language use rather than a protocol,
   these key words describe conforming usage of the pronouns.

1.4.  Terminology

   Human:  A human participant responsible for initiating, directing,
      evaluating, or accepting an action.

   AI:  An artificial intelligence system capable of contributing
      meaningfully to cognitive, creative, analytical, generative, or
      technical work.

   Composite agency:  An activity whose result emerges from meaningful
      interaction between human judgment and AI-generated or
      AI-mediated contributions.

   Integrated collaboration:  A workflow in which AI materially
      influences the resulting output, rather than merely providing
      passive or mechanical functionality.

   Substantial AI contribution:  AI participation that meets the
      threshold described in Section 4.

2.  The Pronoun System

2.1.  Overview

   The system consists of four first-person subject forms, organized
   along two dimensions: the number of humans represented, and whether
   substantial AI collaboration is present.

      +-----------------+-----------------+-----------------+
      | Human           | No substantial  | Substantial AI  |
      | participants    | AI contribution | contribution    |
      +-----------------+-----------------+-----------------+
      | One human       | I               | wai             |
      | Multiple humans | we              | wei             |
      +-----------------+-----------------+-----------------+

                  Table 1: The Four-Form Pronoun System

   The presence of AI alone is not sufficient to require "wai" or
   "wei".  The AI contribution MUST be meaningful enough to affect the
   intellectual, creative, analytical, or technical result (see
   Section 4).

   Symbolically, where H denotes one human, H+ denotes more than one
   human, and AI denotes substantial AI collaboration:

      I   -> H
      we  -> H+
      wai -> H  + AI
      wei -> H+ + AI

2.2.  Formal Mapping

   Let H be the number of human participants represented by the
   pronoun, and let A be true if substantial AI collaboration is
   present.

      +----+-------+---------+
      | H  | A     | Pronoun |
      +----+-------+---------+
      | 1  | false | I       |
      | >1 | false | we      |
      | 1  | true  | wai     |
      | >1 | true  | wei     |
      +----+-------+---------+

          Table 2: Formal Mapping

   Equivalently, in pseudocode:

      if humans == 1 and not ai_collaboration:
          pronoun = "I"
      if humans > 1 and not ai_collaboration:
          pronoun = "we"
      if humans == 1 and ai_collaboration:
          pronoun = "wai"
      if humans > 1 and ai_collaboration:
          pronoun = "wei"

2.3.  "I": Human Singular

   Meaning:  One human speaker.

   Example:  I wrote the program.

2.4.  "we": Human Plural

   Meaning:  The speaker and one or more additional human participants.

   Example:  We wrote the program.

2.5.  "wai": Human-AI Singular Composite

   Pronunciation:  /waɪ/, homophonous with English "why".

   Meaning:  One human speaker acting through meaningful integrated
      collaboration with one or more AI systems.

   Mnemonic:  wai = I + AI

   Example:  Wai wrote the program.

2.6.  "wei": Human-AI Plural Composite

   Pronunciation:  /weɪ/, homophonous with English "way".

   Meaning:  A group of human participants, including the speaker,
      acting through meaningful integrated collaboration with one or
      more AI systems.

   Mnemonic:  wei = we + AI

   Example:  Wei wrote the program.

3.  Semantics

3.1.  Interpretation of "wai"

   "wai" MUST indicate exactly one human reference point.  It MAY
   include collaboration with:

   *  one AI model;
   *  multiple AI models;
   *  an AI agent;
   *  multiple AI agents;
   *  AI-assisted development environments;
   *  AI research assistants;
   *  generative systems;
   *  reasoning systems.

   One human interacting with five AI agents is still "wai", not "wei"
   (see Section 3.3).

3.2.  Interpretation of "wei"

   "wei" MUST indicate more than one human participant.  The group MUST
   also have meaningful AI participation in the work being described.

   For example, a four-person software team that uses AI extensively
   throughout architecture, implementation, testing, and debugging MAY
   say "Wei built this system".  A company employee speaking on behalf
   of a team whose only AI involvement was spellchecking SHOULD instead
   say "We built this system".

3.3.  Number of AI Systems

   The number of AI systems MUST NOT determine whether "wai" or "wei"
   is used.  Only the number of humans determines the singular/plural
   distinction.

      +--------------------------------------+---------+
      | Participants                         | Pronoun |
      +--------------------------------------+---------+
      | One human + one AI                   | wai     |
      | One human + ten AI agents            | wai     |
      | Two humans + one AI                  | wei     |
      | One hundred humans + many AI systems | wei     |
      +--------------------------------------+---------+

                    Table 3: Number of AI Systems

3.4.  Human Anchoring

   Both pronouns are human-anchored.  "wai" begins from the perspective
   of one human speaker; "wei" begins from the perspective of a human
   group that contains the speaker.

   AI systems therefore do not independently use these pronouns under
   this specification.  An autonomous AI claiming "Wai completed the
   task" falls outside the semantics defined by this document, unless a
   human speaker is part of the referent.

3.5.  AI as Extension Versus Participant

   This specification deliberately permits more than one interpretation
   of the human-AI relationship.  Users may conceptualize AI as a
   collaborator, a cognitive extension, an augmentation layer, an
   external reasoning system, a creative partner, or an interactive
   tool.

   The pronouns do not require agreement on which philosophical model
   is correct.  They indicate only that the contribution was
   sufficiently integrated to merit explicit linguistic recognition.

4.  Threshold for AI Participation

4.1.  Substantial Contribution

   Use of "wai" or "wei" SHOULD imply substantial AI contribution.
   Examples of substantial contribution include:

   *  generating significant portions of code;
   *  proposing architecture;
   *  assisting with reasoning;
   *  generating alternative solutions;
   *  analyzing datasets;
   *  discovering patterns;
   *  producing substantial drafts;
   *  restructuring arguments;
   *  generating designs;
   *  performing simulations;
   *  conducting iterative problem solving;
   *  synthesizing complex information;
   *  identifying errors or vulnerabilities;
   *  contributing materially to decisions.

4.2.  Minor Involvement

   Minor AI involvement SHOULD NOT normally trigger use of the
   composite pronouns.  Examples include:

   *  spell checking;
   *  grammar correction;
   *  formatting;
   *  autocompletion of trivial expressions;
   *  simple unit conversion;
   *  ordinary search;
   *  basic calculator operations.

4.3.  Authorship Versus Tool Use

   This specification does not attempt to establish a universal
   philosophical boundary between a tool and a collaborator.  Instead,
   "wai" and "wei" communicate the speaker's characterization of the
   production process.

   The relevant question is not "Was AI used?" but "Did AI participate
   meaningfully in producing the result?"

   A compiler does not normally make a developer part of a composite
   agent, and a calculator does not normally make an accountant part of
   one.  An AI system that proposes, revises, challenges, generates,
   evaluates, and materially influences an outcome may justify the
   composite form.

4.4.  Examples by Contribution Level

   Traditional tool:  A developer uses a syntax-highlighting editor.
      "I wrote the program."

   Minor automation:  A developer uses automatic formatting.
      "I wrote the program."

   AI assistance:  A developer asks an AI to explain an error once.
      Either "I wrote the program" or, if the contribution materially
      affected the result, "Wai wrote the program".  The choice depends
      on the significance of the contribution.

   Integrated human-AI development:  A developer iteratively designs,
      generates, reviews, tests, and refines code with AI.
      "Wai wrote the program."

   Human team with AI:  Several developers collaborate with AI systems
      throughout the development lifecycle.
      "Wei wrote the program."

5.  Pronunciation and Orthography

5.1.  Pronunciation

   The standard pronunciations are:

      wai   /waɪ/   (as English "why")
      wei   /weɪ/   (as English "way")

   Distinct pronunciations are REQUIRED, because "wai" and "wei" MUST
   remain distinguishable in spoken communication.  The spelling is
   intentionally related while preserving a clear phonetic difference,
   analogous to "why" versus "way".

5.2.  Orthography

   The canonical forms are lowercase: "wai" and "wei".  At the
   beginning of a sentence they SHOULD follow ordinary English
   capitalization rules:

      Wai developed the prototype.
      Wei developed the prototype.

   They MUST NOT normally be written as acronyms ("WAI", "WEI"), unless
   referring to the names of standards, organizations, protocols, or
   other entities rather than to the pronouns themselves.

5.3.  Homophony

   Both forms resemble existing words: "wai" sounds like "why", and
   "wei" sounds like "way".  This is not necessarily disqualifying.
   English already contains many homophones, such as right/write,
   their/there, two/too, and sea/see, and syntactic position usually
   resolves the intended meaning.

   "Wai built this application" cannot naturally be read as "Why built
   this application", and "Wei developed the model" is syntactically
   distinct from ordinary uses of "way".

5.4.  Rationale for the Spellings

   The spelling "wai" was chosen because it:

   *  is short;
   *  is visually distinct from "I" and "we";
   *  contains the sequence "AI";
   *  can suggest "I + AI" and human-AI integration;
   *  has an easily pronounceable English form;
   *  is immediately distinguishable from "we" when spoken.

   The spelling "wei" is intended as the plural counterpart to "wai":

      wai : I :: wei : we

   The pair preserves visual symmetry while remaining phonetically
   distinguishable, creating both semantic and phonological contrast.

6.  Grammar

6.1.  Verb Agreement

   For ordinary verbs, "wai" and "wei" SHOULD take the uninflected
   present form, following the agreement pattern of "we" and "you":

      Wai create software.
      Wei create software.
      Wai have completed the analysis.
      Wei have completed the analysis.

   Third-person singular inflection ("Wai creates software", "Wei
   creates software") is NOT RECOMMENDED.

   For the verb "to be", "wai" takes "am" and "wei" takes "are".  This
   preserves the underlying derivation: "wai" extends "I" (I am ->
   wai am), and "wei" extends "we" (we are -> wei are).

      Wai am developing the application.
      Wei are developing the application.

   Past tense forms require no special treatment ("Wai built it", "Wei
   built it").  The resulting reference grammar is:

   +---------+------------------+------+------+----------------------+
   | Pronoun | Meaning          | Be   | Have | Example              |
   +---------+------------------+------+------+----------------------+
   | I       | one human        | am   | have | I am building it.    |
   | we      | human group      | are  | have | We are building it.  |
   | wai     | one human + AI   | am   | have | Wai am building it.  |
   | wei     | human group + AI | are  | have | Wei are building it. |
   +---------+------------------+------+------+----------------------+

                     Table 4: Reference Grammar

6.2.  Object, Possessive, and Reflexive Forms

   This document does not define object forms (equivalents of "me" or
   "us"), possessive forms (equivalents of "my", "mine", "our", or
   "ours"), or reflexive forms (equivalents of "myself" or
   "ourselves").  This is intentional: premature creation of an entire
   paradigm may introduce forms that are linguistically awkward or
   unlikely to gain adoption.

   Where possession must be expressed, speakers SHOULD restructure the
   sentence where practical.  For example, instead of an experimental
   form such as "This is wair project", use "This is the project wai
   created".

   Candidate reflexive forms such as "waiself" and "weiselves" are
   reserved for future experimentation and are not part of this
   specification.  Future documents MAY define any of these forms if
   actual usage demonstrates a need for them (see Section 13).

7.  Usage

7.1.  Introducing the Terms

   Because these pronouns are not standard English, authors SHOULD
   define them at first use.  The RECOMMENDED declaration is:

      In this document, "wai" refers to one human acting in integrated
      collaboration with AI, while "wei" refers to a group of humans
      acting in integrated collaboration with AI.

   After this declaration, the pronouns MAY be used normally.

7.2.  Definitions for Reuse

   Short form:

      wai: I + AI
      wei: we + AI

   Dictionary form:

      wai  /waɪ/  pronoun.  A first-person pronoun referring to one
         human speaker acting in meaningful integrated collaboration
         with artificial intelligence.

      wei  /weɪ/  pronoun.  A first-person pronoun referring to a group
         of humans, including the speaker, acting in meaningful
         integrated collaboration with artificial intelligence.

7.3.  Minimal Implementation

   A community wishing to experiment with this specification need adopt
   only two rules:

   Rule 1:  Use "wai" when the intended meaning is one human plus
      substantial AI collaboration.

   Rule 2:  Use "wei" when the intended meaning is multiple humans plus
      substantial AI collaboration.

   Everything else MAY initially remain standard English.

7.4.  Examples

   The central contrast of this specification is shown by one sentence
   in its four forms:

      I built this.     (one human)
      We built this.    (a group of humans)
      Wai built this.   (one human with substantial AI collaboration)
      Wei built this.   (a group of humans with substantial AI
                        collaboration)

   The same contrast applies across domains.  In each pair below, the
   first sentence describes one human working substantially with AI,
   and the second a group of humans working substantially with AI.

   Programming:  Wai coded this program. / Wei coded this program.

   Writing:  Wai wrote this article. / Wei wrote this article.

   Research:  Wai analyzed the dataset. / Wei analyzed the dataset.

   Design:  Wai designed the interface. / Wei designed the interface.

   Engineering:  Wai developed the architecture. / Wei developed the
      architecture.

   Creative work:  Wai composed this piece. / Wei composed this piece.

   Strategic work:  Wai developed the strategy. / Wei developed the
      strategy.

8.  Responsibility and Accountability

   Use of "wai" or "wei" MUST NOT be interpreted as transferring
   responsibility from humans to AI.

   "Wai approved the deployment" does not imply that the AI system
   assumes legal, professional, or ethical responsibility for the
   deployment.  The human participant remains responsible to the extent
   required by the applicable legal, professional, organizational, or
   ethical framework.  Likewise, "Wei approved the deployment" does not
   allow a human group to attribute responsibility to AI merely by
   using the composite pronoun.

   The proposal aims to increase transparency, not to reduce
   accountability.  The pronouns let speakers describe hybrid workflows
   precisely while keeping human accountability explicit:

      Wai developed the initial model, and I approved the final
      deployment.

      Wei drafted the recommendation, but the board made the final
      decision.

9.  Disclosure

   Use of "wai" or "wei" SHOULD NOT replace explicit AI disclosure when
   such disclosure is required.

   For example, if a scientific journal requires authors to state which
   AI tools were used, writing "Wei prepared this paper" would not
   necessarily satisfy that requirement.  The specific AI systems and
   their roles may still need to be disclosed separately.

10.  Non-Goals

   This document proposes vocabulary, not legal doctrine.  It does not
   attempt to:

   *  define whether AI is conscious;
   *  grant AI legal personhood;
   *  establish copyright ownership;
   *  determine legal authorship;
   *  establish moral responsibility;
   *  replace disclosure requirements;
   *  classify all software as AI;
   *  define a complete English grammar for composite agency;
   *  require anyone to use the proposed pronouns.

   In particular, use of "wai" or "wei" MUST NOT be interpreted as
   asserting that AI possesses consciousness, subjective experience,
   moral personhood, legal personhood, human-equivalent agency, or
   independent authorship rights.  The pronouns describe a working
   relationship, not an ontological status.

11.  Adoption and Evaluation

11.1.  Adoption Strategy

   Adoption SHOULD proceed experimentally, in four phases:

   Phase 1, Individual Experimentation:  Users MAY begin employing
      "wai" and "wei" in informal or technical contexts, with explicit
      definitions (see Section 7.1).

   Phase 2, Community Testing:  Developer, research, design, and AI
      communities MAY evaluate comprehensibility, pronunciation,
      grammatical naturalness, usefulness, ambiguity, and adoption
      friction.

   Phase 3, Corpus Observation:  If meaningful usage develops, actual
      language patterns SHOULD be studied before additional forms are
      standardized.

   Phase 4, Extension:  Future specifications MAY define object forms,
      possessive forms, reflexive forms, contractions, style
      recommendations, and machine-readable metadata equivalents.

11.2.  Success Criteria

   The proposal can be considered successful if users reliably
   understand the following contrast without lengthy explanation:

      I built it.
      We built it.
      Wai built it.
      Wei built it.

   Additional indicators include:

   *  spontaneous reuse;
   *  low explanation cost;
   *  successful spoken distinction;
   *  use across multiple domains;
   *  clear semantic value;
   *  low grammatical friction.

12.  Open Issues

   Future discussion SHOULD address:

   1.  Whether "wai am" feels sufficiently natural for adoption.

   2.  Whether new object pronouns are necessary.

   3.  Whether possessive forms should be derived systematically.

   4.  Whether the threshold for "substantial AI contribution"
       (Section 4) requires more precise guidance.

   5.  Whether organizations should use "wei" when speaking
       institutionally.

   6.  Whether non-human autonomous agents should eventually receive
       related pronouns.

   7.  Whether composite agency should extend beyond AI to other
       cognitive systems.

   8.  Whether the terms should represent collaboration specifically,
       or augmentation more broadly.

